  "pr_number": 2640,
  "issue_number": 2639,
  "issue_title": "feat(engine): implement Ripple keyword runtime (CR 702.60)",
  "issue_body": "## Summary\n\n`Keyword::Ripple` is parsed but is a **unit variant that discards N** and has **zero runtime** \u2014 `grep \"Keyword::Ripple\" crates/engine/src/game/` returns nothing, and there is no `synthesize_ripple`. The parser even self-documents the gap (`oracle_keyword.rs:1279`: *\"Keyword::Ripple is currently a unit variant (N is not yet tracked by the engine)\"*). So on every card with ripple the keyword is a complete no-op: casting it never reveals the top N or casts the same-named copies.\n\nThis is the last inert keyword with real cards. It belongs to the same \"reveal-from-top, cast-during-resolution\" family the engine already implements end-to-end for **Cascade** and **Discover**, so the runtime is largely an assembly of existing building blocks.\n\n## Comprehensive Rules\n\n**CR 702.60a** (verified in `docs/MagicCompRules.txt`): *\"Ripple N\" means \"When you cast this spell, you may reveal the top N cards of your library. You may cast any of those cards with the same name as this spell without paying their mana costs. Then put all revealed cards not cast this way on the bottom of your library in a random order.\"*\n\n**CR 702.60b**: Multiple instances of ripple each trigger separately.\n\n## Why this is valuable\n\n- Covers the **class** of ripple cards (not one card): the Coldsnap \"Surging\" cycle \u2014 **Surging Aether, Surging Dementia, Surging Flame, Surging Might, Surging Sentinels** \u2014 plus **Thrumming Stone**, which grants \"Ripple 4\" to every spell you cast (a combo enabler). Card count: `grep -oc '\"_Rule\":\"Ripple\"' data/mtgish-cards.json` \u2192 5, and Thrumming Stone makes it open-ended.\n- It is the **final** parsed-but-inert keyword with cards; implementing it closes the keyword-runtime coverage gap that Convoke/Improvise/Assist/Delve/Spectacle/Splice/etc. have been filling.\n- **High tractability** \u2014 it reuses the Cascade/Discover pipeline rather than introducing new machinery.\n\n## Implementation plan (mirror Cascade)\n\n1. **Carry N** \u2014 change `Keyword::Ripple` \u2192 `Keyword::Ripple(u32)`, exactly as `Dredge(u32)` does. Update the parser at `oracle_keyword.rs:1277-1289` to capture N (it currently parses then drops it), plus `FromStr`/display/`kind()` sites. (This is the small ripple already noted in the parser comment.)\n2. **Synthesize the trigger** \u2014 add `synthesize_ripple` emitting a **\"when you cast this spell\"** triggered ability (the same SpellCast trigger shape Cascade uses, `triggers.rs:13478` / the Cascade synth path). CR 702.60b multiplicity falls out of the per-instance synthesis the engine already does for Cascade/Storm.\n3. **Resolve via the existing cast-from-exile authority** \u2014 the effect reveals the top N (reuse the `exile_from_top` / `effects/exile_from_top_until.rs` \"Cascade/Discover-shape\" reveal), filters the revealed cards by **same name as the source spell**, and offers casting each match for free through the cast-during-resolution authority (`casting.rs:6595`, CR 608.2g) and the `CastOfferKind` / resolution-choice flow in `engine_resolution_choices.rs`. Non-cast revealed cards go to the **bottom in a random order** (reuse the existing shuffle/bottom primitive).\n4. **\"Same name as this spell\" filter** \u2014 the only mildly novel piece; express it as a `TargetFilter`/name predicate against the source spell's name (the synthesis layer already resolves `SelfRef` to the source).\n5. **AI / FE** \u2014 the cast-offer reuses the existing Cascade/Discover `CastOffer` decision surface, so the AI candidate path and the frontend offer modal already exist; no new `WaitingFor` variant.\n\n## Verification plan\n\n- `cargo fmt --all`, full-workspace `clippy --workspace --exclude phase-tauri --exclude mtgish-import -- -D warnings`, full-workspace `cargo check` (a `Keyword` enum shape change ripples to downstream crates \u2014 verify the whole workspace, not just `-p engine`), engine lib suite.\n- Tests: casting a `Ripple N` spell reveals N and offers casting same-named cards for free; non-matching revealed cards go to the bottom; an empty/again-rippling reveal terminates; Thrumming Stone grants Ripple 4 to an unrelated spell and triggers it; multiple ripple instances trigger separately (CR 702.60b).\n\nNote: tests/clippy run locally via the nightly-GNU toolchain (+ mingw `dlltool` on PATH). The runtime reuses the Cascade/Discover reveal-and-cast pipeline, so the blast radius is the parser N-capture + one new synthesizer.\n\n## Honest scope note\n\nRipple is **5 printed cards** (plus Thrumming Stone's grant) \u2014 modest by card count. It is recommended because it is the last inert keyword *and* the cheapest to ship correctly (it slots into proven infrastructure). The only comparable remaining inert keyword, **Epic (CR 702.50, also ~5 cards)**, needs net-new \"for the rest of the game, copy this spell each upkeep / can't cast spells\" infrastructure with no existing analogue, so it is far less tractable for the same payoff.",
